    "slug": "custom-tissue-paper-guide",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-10",
    "title": "Custom Tissue Paper: Weights, Printing and Specification Data",
    "category": "Sourcing Guide",
    "date": "October 2026",
    "readTime": "4 min read",
    "image": "/images/covers/custom-tissue-paper-guide-reviewed.png",
    "imageAlt": "Illustrative buyer checklist: Specify the paper and print. Confirm project specifications; not a product photograph.",
    "imageWidth": 2400,
    "imageHeight": 1350,
    "excerpt": "Specify tissue purpose, paper, sheet fit and artwork together; distinguish external catalogue examples from the project specification.",
    "metaDescription": "Plan custom tissue paper by purpose, proposed GSM, print process, sheet fit and product contact. Compare the actual paper sample and quotation.",
    "body": "*This guide separates paper selection, printing and component fit. External catalogue examples are attributed to their supplier; they are not ELAPACK stock specifications or printing guarantees.*\n\n> **Quick answer:** specify the tissue's purpose, sheet size, proposed paper and artwork together. GSM measures weight per area; it does not by itself determine print capability, protection, colourfastness or price.\n\n## Define the job before selecting paper\n\nTissue may be proposed as a presentation wrap, a liner or part of an inner packing arrangement. Those jobs need different checks. For presentation, review translucency, fold appearance and logo legibility. For contact with the product, review transfer, abrasion and compatibility on the actual materials. If protection is needed, test the complete pack rather than assuming tissue is a protective insert.\n\nThe [rigid boxes guide](https://elapack.com/news/custom-rigid-boxes-guide/) covers the outer construction; the [packaging inserts guide](https://elapack.com/news/packaging-inserts-guide/) covers retention. Tissue should complement those components rather than substitute for them.\n\n## Paper weight is one field, not a universal ladder\n\nAn external example is [TissueMotif](https://tissuemotif.com/), whose catalogue lists 17 GSM and 28 GSM tissue. Its current homepage presents single-logo-colour options for both weights. These are that supplier's offerings, not an industry rule that one weight permits one colour and another automatically permits full colour.\n\n| Decision | What to request | Sample check |\n|---|---|---|\n| Paper | Proposed GSM, finish, colour and composition | Feel, translucency and fold appearance |\n| Print | Available process, colour count and repeat layout | Fine detail, contrast and placement |\n| Contact | Product surfaces and intended storage | Transfer and visible marking |\n| Sheet fit | Product, box interior and folding sequence | Coverage without unwanted bulk |\n| Packing | Sheet count and packing sequence | Repeatable assembly with the other components |\n\nDo not infer a fixed application or quality tier from GSM alone. Two papers with the same GSM can differ in composition, finish and printing behaviour. Ask for actual paper references and a proposed alternative if the first sample does not meet the brief.\n\n## Confirm printing with the actual artwork\n\nProvide the logo, colour reference, repeat spacing and areas that must remain unprinted. Ask which processes and colour counts are available on the proposed paper. Review a proof for layout and an appropriate physical sample for print and contact behaviour. A digital image cannot establish ink transfer resistance or paper feel.\n\nPrinting cost should be quoted for the same paper, sheet size, artwork and quantity. Neither full colour nor one colour has a universal cost relationship independent of the production method and setup.\n\n## Fit the sheet to the complete presentation\n\nMeasure the intended box interior and the product with its insert. Fold a sample sheet around the actual contents and check opening access, logo position, closure and packing time. Avoid excess material that makes the box difficult to close. Tissue quantity is a separate component field in a set quotation.\n\nFor a [magnetic gift box](https://elapack.com/products/magnetic-closure-gift-box/) or [ring box](https://elapack.com/products/custom-ring-boxes/), confirm the whole packing sequence rather than assuming one sheet size works across both. The [sets collection](https://elapack.com/products/?category=Sets%20%26%20Complete%20Packaging) provides the related multi-component context; confirm tissue availability and specification for the selected project.\n\n## Enquiry and approval\n\nSend the purpose, product-contact surfaces, box dimensions, proposed fold, artwork, sheet quantity and delivery destination. Request the proposed paper and print specification, sample scope, component price and acceptance checks in writing.\n\nELAPACK's MOQ is 200 pieces. Confirm size, colour and artwork allocation in the quotation; this does not mean every variant can be split freely. For a complete set, each component has a 200-piece minimum. Custom samples are USD 25 plus USD 20 shipping, with a 3–5 day sample-making timeframe. Stock samples are free with USD 25 shipping and a stated delivery timeframe of 4–7 days. Production is typically 15–20 days after sample approval and payment receipt; confirm by order.\n\n[Contact ELAPACK](https://elapack.com/contact/) with the box and tissue brief. Confirm tissue-specific availability, sheet unit and any project-specific sample conditions in the quotation."
